.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt SYMLINK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm symlink ,
.Nm symlinkat ,
.Nm readlink ,
.Nm readlinkat
.Nd create or read a symbolic link
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn symlink "const char *target" "const char *linkpath"
.Ft int
.Fn symlinkat "const char *target" "int dirfd" "const char *linkpath"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn readlink "const char *restrict path" "char *restrict buf" "size_t bufsiz"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn readlinkat "int dirfd" "const char *restrict path" "char *restrict buf" "size_t bufsiz"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn symlink
creates
.Fa linkpath
with the symbolic-link text
.Fa target .
The target is stored without resolving it and need not exist.
An existing destination, including a dangling symbolic link, is not
replaced.
An empty target is refused.
A
.Fa linkpath
that names a directory by its end, which no symbolic link is, makes
nothing, as on Linux: with a slash after its last name it returns
.Er ENOENT
when nothing has that name and
.Er EEXIST
when something does, and with a last component of
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
it returns
.Er EEXIST ,
as the root does.
These come before the refusals of a read-only mount, of
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc ,
and of a file system without symbolic links.
.Pp
ext2 and tmpfs, whose mounts include
.Pa /dev/shm ,
support creation of symbolic links.
The parent directory must permit writing, and path resolution requires
search permission on ancestor directories.
A read-only mount or synthetic namespace returns
.Er EROFS .
Writable KRFFS, FAT32, and exFAT mounts return
.Er EPERM
because they have no symbolic-link creation operation.
.Pp
.Fn readlink
copies up to
.Fa bufsiz
bytes of a link's text into
.Fa buf
and returns the number copied.
It does not follow the final link or append a terminating null byte,
except for a path that ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
which names a directory: the final link is followed then, and the call
returns
.Er EINVAL
for the directory it reaches and
.Er ENOTDIR
for anything else, as on Linux.
A short buffer truncates the text without an error.
A return equal to
.Fa bufsiz
does not distinguish an exact fit from truncation.
The caller must provide its own terminator when using the result as
a C string.
.Pp
Besides ext2 and tmpfs links,
.Fn readlink
supports
.Pa /proc/self ,
whose text is the caller's numeric process ID, and
.Pa /proc/<pid>/fd/<n> ,
whose text describes that process's open descriptor.
Reading an ordinary file or a node on a file system without a readlink
operation returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The raw kernel readlink calls reject a null result pointer or a zero
buffer size with
.Er EINVAL .
For a zero size, musl instead supplies a private one-byte buffer and
returns zero if the read succeeds.
.Ss Relative paths and limits
.Fn symlinkat
resolves a relative
.Fa linkpath
from
.Fa dirfd ;
.Fn readlinkat
does the same for
.Fa path .
.Dv AT_FDCWD
(-100) selects the current working directory.
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
Other descriptors must refer to a directory and are converted to its
path; ancestor search permissions still apply.
The descriptor does not change the interpretation of the stored
.Fa target .
.Pp
When a link is followed, an absolute target is resolved from the root
and a relative target from the directory containing the link.
At most 40 links are followed while resolving one path; a forty-first
returns
.Er ELOOP .
.Pp
The copied link pathname, including a current-directory or descriptor
prefix and terminating null byte, must fit 4096 bytes.
.Fn symlink
and
.Fn symlinkat
copy at most 4095 target bytes; ext2 stores at most a block less one
byte, 4095 on the volumes this kernel writes, and tmpfs keeps a target
of any of those lengths in kernel memory.
A target combined with the remaining path during resolution must also
fit 4095 bytes, excluding the terminator; Linux has no such bound on the
combination.
.Fn readlink
reads a target into an internal buffer of 4096 bytes, which fits every
target ext2 or tmpfs stores.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers and register arguments are:
.Bl -tag -width "readlinkat (305)"
.It Nm symlink No (83)
EBX is the target pointer and ECX the new link pathname.
.It Nm symlinkat No (304)
EBX is the target pointer, ECX the directory descriptor, and EDX the
new link pathname.
.It Nm readlink No (85)
EBX is the path pointer, ECX the buffer, and EDX its size.
.It Nm readlinkat No (305)
EBX is the directory descriptor, ECX the path pointer, EDX the buffer,
and ESI its size.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn symlink
and
.Fn symlinkat
return 0 on success.
.Fn readlink
and
.Fn readlinkat
return the number of bytes copied.
On failure these functions return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/procprobe.c
reads the proc descriptor link for a socket, appends a null byte itself,
and checks that the reported inode agrees with
.Xr fstat64 2
and
.Pa /proc/net/tcp .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission on an ancestor directory or write permission on the
new link's parent was denied.
.It Bq Er EBADF
A relative path uses an invalid directory descriptor.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
The new link's name already exists or is the root, or its last
component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A pathname, target, or result buffer cannot be accessed as required,
a required pathname or target pointer is null, or there is no current
process.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
The ext2 parent directory cannot grow further or a required block
exceeds the addressing limit.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The object to read is not a symbolic link, the file system has no
readlink operation, or a raw kernel readlink call has a null buffer or
zero size.
.It Bq Er EIO
File-system I/O or metadata validation failed, a stored link is malformed,
or a mount's device was removed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A copied or reconstructed path, component, target, or link expansion
exceeds its buffer.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A pathname or creation target is empty, a required component is absent,
the new link's name has a slash after it and nothing has that name, or
the process or descriptor referred to by a proc link no longer exists.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A required file-system node or the text buffer of a proc link cannot be
allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The file system has no inode or data space available for the link or
its directory entry.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component or relative-path base descriptor is not a directory,
or a path to read that ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The writable file system does not support symbolic-link creation.
.It Bq Er EROFS
Creation was requested on a read-only mount or in a synthetic namespace.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr link 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
